            <h1>Getting started with PanelsDCC</h1>
            <p>PanelsDCC is <strong>not</strong> a traditional desktop application, neither is it an iOS or Android app. Configuration is cloud based while control is local and accessible from any device with a web browser connected to PanelsDCC Control.</p>
            <p><strong>What you need</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>A Raspberry&nbsp;Pi</strong> on your layout, with PanelsDCC Control and PanelsDCC Connect installed.</li>
                <li><strong>A supported DCC command station</strong>, physically connected to the Raspberry&nbsp;Pi. Currently <a href="https://dcc-ex.com/ex-csb1/index.html#gsc.tab=0" target="_blank">DCC-EX</a> (via USB) and <a href="https://uk.hornby.com/products/elite-digital-controller-r8214" target="_blank">Hornby Elite (via USB)</a> are known working. See <a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/connect.html">Connecting your DCC controller/s</a> for more information.</li>
                <li><strong>Your own devices</strong> – phones, tablets, laptops, desktops – with a modern web browser to connect to the Raspberry&nbsp;Pi over your network.</li>
                <li><strong>A PanelsDCC account</strong> so you can configure trains, accessories, and panels.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Step 1: Install PanelsDCC Connect and Control</h2>
            <p>Install both apps on your Raspberry Pi — either from a fresh OS image or on an existing Raspberry Pi OS system.</p>

            <h3>Prerequisites</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Raspberry Pi 4 or later</li>
                <li>16&nbsp;GB microSD card</li>
                <li>PC with microSD reader (or adapter)</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Option A: New SD card with Raspberry Pi Imager</h3>
            <p>Follow the standard <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/" target="_blank">Raspberry Pi Imager</a> flow. After you pick your Raspberry Pi, Imager will recommend an OS. Choose the <strong>64-bit</strong> option if it is available. Pick a <strong>Desktop</strong> image if you want a screen, keyboard, and mouse on the Pi, or if you might use the desktop over a remote connection. Choose the version without Desktop if you never intend to plug in a monitor or use the desktop remotely.</p>
            <p><strong>In Imager, step by step:</strong></p>
            <ol>
                <li>Open Raspberry Pi Imager.</li>
                <li>Choose your Raspberry Pi device.</li>
                <li>Choose the OS Imager recommends — 64-bit if available, with Desktop if you want it, without Desktop if you will never use a monitor or remote desktop.</li>
                <li>Choose your SD card, then click <strong>Next</strong>.</li>
                <li>When asked about OS customisation, click <strong>Edit Settings</strong>.</li>
                <li>On the <strong>General</strong> tab, set hostname, username/password, Wi‑Fi, locale, and timezone.</li>
                <li>For hostname, we recommend <strong>panels-dcc</strong> so your URLs are easy to remember.</li>
                <li>On the <strong>Services</strong> tab, enable SSH if you want remote access (optional).</li>
                <li>Save settings and write the image to the card. Do not put the card in the Pi yet.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Before first boot:</strong></p>
            <ol>
                <li>Download the PanelsDCC vendor-data file:</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="install-download"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= basePath %>/vendor-data" download="vendor-data">Download vendor-data</a></p>
            <ol start="2">
                <li>Imager may eject the card when it finishes. If it does, unplug the card and plug it back in, then open the SD card’s boot partition on this computer. It is usually labelled <strong>bootfs</strong>. This folder should contain files like <code>user-data</code> and <code>meta-data</code>.</li>
                <li>Copy the file there as exactly <strong>vendor-data</strong> (no filename extension). If your browser saved it as <code>vendor-data.txt</code>, rename it.</li>
                <li>Eject the card, put it in the Pi, and boot. For install to succeed the Pi must be able to reach the internet — plug it into a router, or make sure it joins the Wi‑Fi you set in Imager.</li>
                <li>Leave the Pi running. First boot updates the OS, installs PanelsDCC, and may reboot once. This can take 15 to 30 minutes.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>There is no on-screen “finished” message. After waiting, go to <strong>Step 2</strong> — if Connect and Control open in the browser, the install has succeeded. If they do not load yet, wait a bit longer and try again.</p>

            <h3>Option B: Pi already running Raspberry Pi OS</h3>
            <p>If the Pi is already set up, install the latest <strong>PanelsDCC Connect</strong> and <strong>PanelsDCC Control</strong> with one command:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Ensure the Pi has internet access.</li>
                <li>Open a terminal on the Pi (or SSH in).</li>
                <li>Run:</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="install-command-block">
                <pre class="install-command" tabindex="0"><code class="install-command-code">curl -fsSL https://panelsd.cc/install.sh | sudo bash</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p>The script downloads and installs everything automatically. Then continue to <strong>Step 2</strong>.</p>

            <h2>Step 2: Post-install checks</h2>
            <%- include('local-panels-urls.ejs', {
                localPanelsHeading: 'Post-install: open in your browser',
                localPanelsLead: 'On a phone, laptop, or the Pi — stay on the same network as the Raspberry Pi. Confirm both Connect (port 9000) and Control (default port) load before continuing.'
            }) %>
            <p>Once that works, continue to <a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/wifi.html">Configure Wi‑Fi</a>, then the <a href="<%= basePath %>/manual/index.html">manual</a> getting-started path (controllers, first train, <a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/account.html">your PanelsDCC account</a>). When that checklist is complete, move on to <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/index.html">Chapter 2 — Configuration</a> on the Panels DCC website.</p>

            <h2>Step 3: Using the Pi directly (optional)</h2>
            <p>If you prefer not to use another device on the network, you can run everything on the Pi itself.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Connect a screen, keyboard, and mouse to the Pi.</li>
                <li>After install, use the desktop shortcuts for <strong>PanelsDCC Connect</strong> and <strong>PanelsDCC Control</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>That is especially useful for changing network setup in Connect (for example switching between home Wi‑Fi and hotspot mode).</p>

